      title: "The Prisoner from Antioch",
      body: `## The third city of the empire

**Antioch**, on the Orontes River in Syria, was one of the great cities of the Roman Empire, ranking after Rome and Alexandria. Founded by one of Alexander the Great's generals around 300 BC, it was the capital of the Roman province of Syria, a city of perhaps a quarter of a million people, with a large and long-established Jewish community. It was in Antioch, according to the book of Acts, that followers of Jesus first preached to Gentiles in large numbers, and there "the disciples were first called **Christians**" (Acts 11:26). Paul and Barnabas set out from Antioch on their missionary journeys, and there Paul confronted Peter over whether Jewish and Gentile believers could eat together (Galatians 2:11–14). The Gospel of Matthew may have been written there or nearby.

Early in the second century, the bishop of the church at Antioch was a man named **Ignatius**, who also called himself **Theophoros**, "God-bearer." Later tradition made him the second or third bishop of Antioch after the apostle Peter. We know almost nothing about his life before his arrest. We know him through seven letters he wrote in the last weeks of his life, on his way to die.

## The road to Rome

Ignatius was arrested in Antioch, for reasons he does not explain, and condemned to die in the arena in Rome, thrown to the wild beasts. Some scholars have suggested that his arrest followed disturbances within the Antiochene church, or between it and its neighbors; Ignatius later learned with joy that the church at Antioch was again "at peace," which may mean that internal quarrels had been resolved. We cannot know. Roman authorities sometimes sent condemned prisoners from the provinces to Rome to provide spectacles in the games, and this seems to have been Ignatius's fate.

He traveled under military guard, overland across Asia Minor. "From Syria to Rome," he wrote, "I am fighting with wild beasts, by land and sea, by night and day, bound to ten leopards—that is, a detachment of soldiers—who only grow worse when they are treated kindly." Along the way, he was met by Christians of the cities on his route. At **Smyrna**, on the Aegean coast, where the guards halted for some time, he was welcomed by the church and its young bishop, **Polycarp**, whom we will meet in the next lesson. There delegations came to him from three other churches of Asia Minor: from **Ephesus**, led by its bishop Onesimus; from **Magnesia**, led by its young bishop Damas; and from **Tralles**, whose bishop Polybius came alone. From Smyrna, Ignatius wrote letters to each of these three churches, thanking them and instructing them. He also wrote a fourth letter, to the church in **Rome**, to prepare it for his arrival.

From Smyrna, the guards took Ignatius north to **Troas**, on the coast near ancient Troy. From there he wrote three more letters: to the church of **Philadelphia**, which he had passed through on his way; to the church of **Smyrna**; and a personal letter to **Polycarp**. At Troas he learned that peace had been restored to the church in Antioch, and he urged the churches to send messengers to congratulate it. Then he was taken across to Macedonia, through the city of **Philippi**, where the Christians welcomed him, and on along the Roman road toward the Adriatic and Italy.

There his story ends. Polycarp, writing to the Philippians shortly afterward, asked them for any reliable news of Ignatius and his companions, and spoke of him as one of those who "are with the Lord, with whom they also suffered." Later tradition held that he was killed by beasts in the Colosseum. No contemporary account of his death survives.

## When?

The traditional date of Ignatius's death, based on Eusebius, is during the reign of the emperor **Trajan** (98–117), and scholars have usually placed it around 110. But Eusebius had no firm evidence, and the letters themselves give no date. Some scholars have argued for a later date, in the 120s or even the 140s—the historian **Timothy Barnes** argued for the 140s—on the grounds that the opponents Ignatius describes resemble the Gnostic teachers of the mid-second century. Most still place the letters in the first decades of the second century, between about 105 and 135.

## A man in a hurry

The seven letters were written quickly, under great stress, by a prisoner who knew he was about to die. They are short, intense, and often difficult. Ignatius writes in a vivid, rushing Greek, full of piled-up phrases, abrupt changes of thought, and striking images. He was not a systematic theologian, and he did not write to set out a theology. He wrote to thank the churches that had welcomed him, to warn them against false teachers, to urge them to unity around their bishops, and to beg the Romans not to save him.

Yet the letters have had an influence out of all proportion to their length. They are our earliest clear evidence for a church led by a single bishop. They contain the first use of the phrases "the catholic church" and "Christianity." They present a theology of Christ, of the Eucharist, and of martyrdom that would shape later Christian thought. And they reveal, as few early Christian writings do, the passionate inner life of a man facing death.

## Ignatius and Paul

Ignatius admired **Paul**, whom he calls "the sanctified, the approved, the blessed," and in whose footsteps he hoped to follow to martyrdom. Like Paul, he wrote letters to churches while a prisoner in chains; and he seems to have known several of Paul's letters, especially 1 Corinthians, whose language he echoes repeatedly. Some scholars think that the collection of Paul's letters may have helped inspire the collection of Ignatius's own. "I do not command you as Peter and Paul did," he wrote to the Romans. "They were apostles; I am a condemned man. They were free; I am even now a slave." But he thought of himself as continuing their work, and of his chains as "spiritual pearls."

> **Pause and consider.** Ignatius's letters were written in a few weeks, by a prisoner under guard, in the shadow of death. How might these circumstances shape what he wrote, and how should they shape the way we read him?`,
